{
  "$comment": [
    "The calibration corpus, pinned by commit so the study is re-runnable.",
    "",
    "TWO POPULATIONS, and the reason there have to be two.",
    "",
    "  kind=maintained           Well-known, actively maintained projects with",
    "                            their own security processes. These set where",
    "                            the *good* end of the scale sits.",
    "",
    "  kind=vulnerable-by-design Training applications written to contain real",
    "                            vulnerabilities \u2014 OWASP NodeGoat, railsgoat,",
    "                            WebGoat, PyGoat, and Juice Shop. These set the",
    "                            *bad* end.",
    "",
    "The first version of this corpus had only the maintained half, and said",
    "so: 'these are all well-maintained OSS projects with their own security",
    "processes. The distribution measured here is cleaner than the population",
    "this tool will actually be pointed at.' That bias is exactly what blocked",
    "D5's band edges. A band table cannot be chosen from a distribution with no",
    "bad end in it \u2014 there is nothing to calibrate D and F against, and any",
    "edge placed down there is a number somebody liked.",
    "",
    "Selection criteria for the maintained half, and the reason for each:",
    "  - Spans the languages the floor actually reads. Calibrating on Python",
    "    and applying the result to Java would be the same error D11 and D12",
    "    made about rule coverage.",
    "  - Spans two orders of magnitude of size, because the normalizer is an",
    "    invented one and a corpus of similarly-sized repositories cannot tell",
    "    us whether it works.",
    "  - Follows the shape MA used at 0.5.0 (django, pytest, black, tornado,",
    "    httpx, lodash, svelte, fastapi).",
    "",
    "The vulnerable half is never executed. Every one of these is cloned,",
    "read from disk by the scanners, and nothing else. They are training",
    "applications, not malware, and WebGoat in particular is included to stay",
    "unexamined on purpose: D12 records that no offline SAST in the floor",
    "reads Java, and a deliberately-vulnerable Java application reporting a",
    "clean score is the sharpest available demonstration of why coverage is",
    "reported beside the grade and never folded into it."
  ],
